    "redesign_cost_faq": {
      "type": "collapsible-content",
      "blocks": {
        "row_1": {
          "type": "collapsible_row",
          "settings": {
            "heading": "How much does it cost to resize a ring?",
            "icon": "none",
            "row_content": "<h5>Most resizing runs <strong>$50&ndash;$180</strong>. Sizing a plain gold band down is $40&ndash;$80; sizing up is $80&ndash;$150, because metal has to be added. Sterling silver is $20&ndash;$60. Platinum runs $50&ndash;$150 and often half again as much as gold. Rings with pav&eacute;, side stones or engraving run $150&ndash;$300 and up. Bay Area bench labor runs roughly 15&ndash;25% above the national average.</h5>"
          }
        },
        "row_2": {
          "type": "collapsible_row",
          "settings": {
            "heading": "Why does sizing up cost more than sizing down?",
            "icon": "none",
            "row_content": "<h5>Because of the metal. Sizing down means cutting a section out, the metal that comes out is scrap the jeweler keeps. Sizing up means cutting the shank and adding a piece of matching metal, which has to be bought. With gold where it is now, that difference is real money.</h5>"
          }
        },
        "row_3": {
          "type": "collapsible_row",
          "settings": {
            "heading": "Is resizing a platinum ring different from gold?",
            "icon": "none",
            "row_content": "<h5>Yes, genuinely. Gold is soldered with a torch. Platinum melts at close to 1,770&deg;C against gold's 1,060&deg;C, so it needs platinum solder at far higher heat, or a laser. A laser puts heat exactly where you point it, so I can weld within a millimeter of a set stone without that heat traveling to reach it. For a platinum ring with stones still set, that's often the difference between sizing it safely and having to unset and reset everything.</h5>"
          }
        },
        "row_4": {
          "type": "collapsible_row",
          "settings": {
            "heading": "What actually happens when a ring is resized?",
            "icon": "none",
            "row_content": "<h5>Metal comes out, or metal goes in. And here's the part that gets mentioned least: <strong>when the metal moves, the settings move with it.</strong> Every prong and bead holds its stone under tension that was set when the ring was its original size. So the work isn't finished when the ring is the right size, it's finished when every stone has been checked and re-tightened.</h5>"
          }
        },
        "row_5": {
          "type": "collapsible_row",
          "settings": {
            "heading": "Can every ring be resized?",
            "icon": "none",
            "row_content": "<h5>No, and the test is simpler than people expect: <strong>is there plain, unset metal at the bottom of the shank?</strong> That's where a jeweler cuts. A plain band or a solitaire with a smooth shank is straightforward. If stones run all the way around, there's nowhere to cut without going through the structure holding them.</h5>"
          }
        },
        "row_6": {
          "type": "collapsible_row",
          "settings": {
            "heading": "Why can't eternity bands be resized?",
            "icon": "none",
            "row_content": "<h5>Because the stones <em>are</em> the band. There's no quiet stretch of metal to open up, and cutting into it means cutting through the settings themselves. Your realistic options are wearing it on a different finger, or having it remade at your size using your own stones.</h5>"
          }
        },
        "row_7": {
          "type": "collapsible_row",
          "settings": {
            "heading": "How many sizes can a ring safely move?",
            "icon": "none",
            "row_content": "<h5>Less than people hope. A plain band can travel a couple of sizes. Once there are stones set into the shank you're realistically looking at half a size to one size. Beyond about two sizes, even on a plain band, you start thinning and stressing the shank where it was joined.</h5>"
          }
        },
        "row_8": {
          "type": "collapsible_row",
          "settings": {
            "heading": "What if my ring won't clear my knuckle?",
            "icon": "none",
            "row_content": "<h5>If your knuckle is larger than the base of your finger, which happens with arthritis, and happens to plenty of us simply with time, a ring sized to slide over the knuckle will spin once it's on. The fix is a fitted shank: either a hinged shank that opens, or sizing beads added inside the band. A plain resize won't solve it.</h5>"
          }
        },
        "row_9": {
          "type": "collapsible_row",
          "settings": {
            "heading": "Should I resize my ring or redesign it?",
            "icon": "none",
            "row_content": "<h5>It depends on one question: <em>would you wear it if it fit?</em> If you love it, size it and enjoy it. If it's inherited, or from a marriage that ended, and you wouldn't wear it anyway, resizing spends money on a ring that goes back in the drawer at a better size. You already own the diamond and the gold, the two most expensive parts of any piece. <a href=\"/pages/jewelry-redesign-cost\">Here's what a redesign costs</a>.</h5>"
          }
        }
      },
